    "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/assets/46929b27eb98506479401e2b78ea18fac539a11dcda4ecfb7b90c700dec61fdb.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
    "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(1,-3),$ and passes through the point $(2,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y+3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,-4),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -1=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+3=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$",
